  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「オジギソウ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%82%AA%E3%82%B8%E3%82%AE%E3%82%BD%E3%82%A6",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 111321066,
      "text": "オジギソウ は、オジギソウ連の植物の一種。別名はネムリグサ（眠り草）、ミモザ。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Mimosa pudica」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mimosa_pudica",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1375640672,
      "text": "Mimosa pudica is a creeping annual or perennial flowering plant of the pea/legume family Fabaceae. It is often grown for its curiosity value: the sensitive compound leaves quickly fold inward and droop when touched or shaken and re-open a few minutes later. For this reason, this species is commonly cited as an example of rapid plant movement. Like a number of other plant species, it undergoes changes in leaf orientation termed \"sleep\" or nyctinastic movement. The foliage closes during darkness and reopens in light. This was first studied by French scientist Jean-Jacques d'Ortous. In the UK it has gained the Royal Horticultural Society's Award of Garden Merit."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Mimosa pudica」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mimosa_pudica#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1375640672,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "In cultivation, this plant is most often grown as an indoor short lived perennial but is also grown for groundcover. Propagation is generally by seed. Germination should take place in 2-3 weeks. Mimosa pudica grows most effectively in nutrient-poor soil that allows for substantial water drainage. However, this plant is also shown to grow in scalped and eroded subsoils. Typically, disrupted soil is necessary in order for M. pudica to become established in an area. Additionally, the plant is shade intolerant and frost-sensitive, meaning that it does not tolerate low levels of light or cold temperatures. Mimosa pudica does not compete for resources with larger foliage or forest canopy undergrowth.\nIn temperate zones it must be grown under protection, where the temperature falls below 13 °C (55 °F).",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
